---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { scales, path, methods } from '../data/site';
---

<Base title="Research">
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow"><b>●</b> Research program</p>
      <h1 class="page-title">Diasporic<br />spatiotemporal<br />genomics</h1>
      <p>
        How moments in history have changed the trajectory of admixture and human variation across the globe, read
        from historical and modern genomes together with the written record.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap two">
      <p class="big-quote">
        How do moments in history change the <em>trajectory of human variation</em> — and how well can we measure it?
      </p>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          DNA is a historical document, and it says the most when it is read next to actual ones. I put historical
          and modern genomes in conversation with legal codes and archival records to ask how particular moments in
          history redirected admixture and population variation, and what signatures they left behind.
        </p>
        <p>
          The slave trades are my model system. The transatlantic trade carried African people into societies that
          went on to draw racial lines in very different ways. In the United States, Black Codes and the one-drop
          rule placed anyone with any African ancestry in a single category. In the Caribbean and South America,
          caste systems graded people by degree of African ancestry, and people of mixed ancestry were treated
          differently from those who were not. Rules like these governed who could form families with whom, so they
          should have sent variation down different paths. The eastern trades across the Sahara, the Red Sea and the
          Indian Ocean, shaped by Islamic law and society, offer a further comparison.
        </p>
        <p>
          Answering these questions takes better tools. Most population-history methods were built for old,
          well-separated groups, and they struggle with populations that are highly admixed and recently diverged. I
          work on improving them, because admixture is the future of our species as human populations become more
          globalized.
        </p>
        <p>
          Genomes record ancestry; the epigenome records response. I use epigenomic data to study plasticity: how
          people are actively reacting, biologically, to changing environments.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Three scales</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="scales">
        {
          scales.map((s) => (
            <article class="scale">
              <div class="num">{s.n}</div>
              <div class="kicker">{s.kicker}</div>
              <h3>{s.title}</h3>
              <p>{s.body}</p>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>The path here</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="path">
        {
          path.map((p) => (
            <li>
              <div>
                <h3>{p.place}</h3>
                <div class="what">{p.what}</div>
              </div>
              <p>{p.detail}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section tint">
    <div class="wrap two">
      <h2>Methods I use and build</h2>
      <ul class="chips">
        {methods.map((m) => <li class="plain">{m}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
